<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>The point equidistant from three points</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A water tank is to be the same distance from three homes at \(P({@P[1]@}, {@P[2]@})\), \(Q({@Qp[1]@}, {@Qp[2]@})\) and \(R({@R[1]@}, {@R[2]@})\). Grid units are metres.</p><p>(a) Give the position of the tank.</p><div>\(\big(\)[[input:ans1]], [[input:ans2]]\(\big)\) [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) How far is it from each home?</p><div>[[input:ans3]] m [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) A point equidistant from \(P\) and \(Q\) is on the perpendicular bisector of \(PQ\); a point equidistant from \(Q\) and \(R\) is on the perpendicular bisector of \(QR\). The tank is where they meet. Writing the tank as \((h, k)\), the two conditions \(TP^2 = TQ^2\) and \(TQ^2 = TR^2\) are two linear equations in \(h\) and \(k\), whose solution is \(({@O[1]@}, {@O[2]@})\).</p><p>(b) \(TP^2 = ({@P[1]-O[1]@})^2 + ({@P[2]-O[2]@})^2 = {@r^2@}\), so the tank is \({@r@}\) m from each home. (Check: \(TQ^2\) and \(TR^2\) are also \({@r^2@}\).)</p><p>The centroid \(({@G[1]@}, {@G[2]@})\), the mean of the three points, is a different point: it is not equidistant from the homes.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
